    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Works with threaders"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Each person finds their comfort tool. Some mix them, floss threaders on weekdays, water flossers when tired. No rulebook here, just what keeps your braces clean."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Tips to Make Flossing Easier"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You don’t need perfection, just consistency. Try these small tricks that make the process easier."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Floss at night when you’re calmer. Morning rushes only lead to skipping."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Keep your elbows close while you work; it steadies your hand."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Use floss threaders made of flexible nylon; they don’t poke."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "For tight gaps, choose dental tape or thin waxed floss."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Rinse before and after to wash out food particles stuck near brackets."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Combine an electric toothbrush with flossing for complete care."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Feels strange the first few nights, but after a week, you’ll floss faster than you think."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "The Importance of Using Waxed or Specialized Floss"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "With braces, the type of floss matters as much as the act itself. Waxed floss doesn’t fray against wires, and unwaxed floss can tear mid-way. A smooth glide reduces friction and avoids irritation around sensitive gums."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Dental tape works well if your teeth have tight spaces, while string floss offers more precision for small corners. Mix and see what works best. The goal isn’t fancy tools, it’s making sure you remove plaque every day."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Traditional Flossing vs Water Flosser"
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/img/blogs/floss2.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Both clean well, but they serve different moods. Traditional flossing with floss threaders gives control, you feel each gap, each clean stroke. Water flossers are quicker, especially for people who wear braces and get tired of threading daily."
    },
    {
      "type": "th",
      "text": "Comparison"
    },
    {
