    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "If possible, combine both. Start with the water flosser to remove food particles, finish with traditional floss for removing plaque near the gum line. It’s the cleanest combo."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Why Skipping Flossing Isn’t Worth It"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "It’s easy to think brushing is enough. But skipping flossing allows debris to stay trapped where the toothbrush can’t reach. That buildup turns into tartar, leading to gum inflammation and eventually tooth decay."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Skipping once or twice won’t ruin your smile, but making it a habit will. Clean brackets, clean wires, clean teeth, that’s what keeps your smile shining after the braces come off."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "So, even on tired nights, give it those few minutes. Your future self will thank you."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Small Orthodontic Tips For Daily Care"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Orthodontic tips that most people learn late:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Always floss before brushing, it clears the path for your toothbrush."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "If your gums bleed mildly at first, it’s normal. Keep going gently."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "If bleeding continues for weeks, consult your dentist."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Carry a small floss threader pack in your bag."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Don’t forget the next tooth just because it looks clean."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Reward yourself after every week of consistency. Tiny celebrations help."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Feels funny sometimes, but that’s what real daily care looks like, small persistence, not perfection."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "How Often Should You Floss With Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Ideally, floss daily. If that’s tough, start with every alternate night. The goal is progress, not pressure. Floss regularly till it becomes routine. Once your fingers adapt, you’ll take less time."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "For a full oral hygiene routine, combine:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Two rounds of brushing using a soft toothbrush"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Flossing tools to clear between teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Rinsing with plain water or mild mouthwash"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You don’t need fancy gadgets, just consistency."
    },
    {
